   cdssconsole:{
    practicalNo:'Module 3 · Unit 1 · Practical 1',
    title:'CDSS Alert Management Console',
    principleNote:'<b>Remember:</b> the severity badge on an alert is a <b>generic rule firing</b>, not a clinical judgement about your patient. The same rule fires identically for a stable outpatient and for someone already bleeding. Only the patient record tells you which one you have.',
    methodNote:'<b>Golden rule:</b> <b>never decide an alert you have not investigated.</b> Two of the ten alerts in this console reverse completely once the record is opened — one over-graded alert that should be overridden, and one under-graded alert that is actively harming the patient. Both look identical from the badge alone.',
    aim:'To work a live Clinical Decision Support System alert queue as the on-call clinical pharmacist — investigating each alert against the patient record, judging its true clinical severity, taking the correct action, and managing alert fatigue without dismissing the alerts that matter.',
    principle:'A CDSS links <b>patient-specific data</b> to a <b>knowledge base</b> to produce advice at the point of care. Its value depends entirely on <b>specificity</b>: a rule that fires on every theoretical interaction, regardless of dose, route, duration or patient factors, produces far more noise than signal. When most alerts are irrelevant, clinicians begin to dismiss them reflexively — <b>alert fatigue</b> — and the occasional critical alert is dismissed along with the rest. Published override rates for interaction alerts commonly exceed 90%, which means the safety net is, in practice, mostly not being read. The pharmacist’s role in this system is therefore not to clear the queue but to act as the <b>specificity layer the software lacks</b>: to take each generic rule, resolve it against this patient’s allergies, current medicines, renal function, laboratory results and clinical condition, and convert it into one of a small number of defensible actions. Every decision must carry a recorded justification, because an override without a reason is indistinguishable from a click, and because those recorded reasons are the evidence base for refining the rules later.',
    theory:'Alerts fall into recognisable categories, and each has a characteristic failure mode. <b>Drug–allergy</b> alerts are the strongest candidates for a hard stop, since documented anaphylaxis to a drug class is an absolute contraindication — piperacillin is a penicillin, and no clinical urgency justifies giving it to a patient who has previously required adrenaline. <b>Drug–drug interaction</b> alerts are where clinical judgement matters most, because relevance depends on dose, duration and the patient’s current state: paracetamol with warfarin is real but dose- and duration-dependent, and irrelevant for PRN use, whereas amiodarone with warfarin is frequently under-graded by systems as “moderate” despite its very long half-life and its capacity to push an INR to dangerous levels over weeks. <b>Renal dosing</b> alerts are commonly under-appreciated: treatment-dose enoxaparin at an eGFR below 30 must be reduced to once daily, and given twice daily it accumulates silently over several days before causing major haemorrhage. <b>Therapeutic duplication</b> alerts usually reveal a medication reconciliation failure rather than a prescribing error. <b>Teratogenicity</b> alerts, notably valproate in a woman of childbearing potential, are governed by regulatory programmes with mandatory documented conditions and are not a matter of individual judgement. Finally, a large proportion of alerts are <b>false positives generated by stale data</b> — most often an unreconciled medication list flagging a drug the patient stopped weeks ago — or are <b>low-value formulary prompts</b> with no safety content at all. Recognising these and overriding them promptly, with the reason recorded, is as much a part of good practice as intercepting the critical ones, because unnecessary delay to needed treatment is itself a harm.',
    requirements:['Live CDSS alert queue with ten alerts arriving over a simulated on-call shift','Patient record for each alert — allergies, current medicines, laboratory results and conditions, all closed until opened','Severity vocabulary — critical, significant, low, not clinically relevant','Action vocabulary — stop and contact prescriber, recommend a change, allow with monitoring, override with reason','Justification list for recording the basis of each decision','Alert Fatigue Index and Patient Safety Index, both live','20-minute shift clock','24-hour outcome projection and printable shift report with QR verification'],
    procedure:['<b>Accept the shift.</b> Alerts begin arriving immediately and continue throughout; the queue is visible on the left at all times.','<b>Open the alert.</b> Note what the system claims the severity is — and treat that as a hypothesis, not a conclusion.','<b>Investigate the record.</b> Open the sections relevant to this alert. Allergies, medicines, labs and conditions are each closed until clicked, and what you open is recorded.','<b>Judge the true clinical severity</b> for this patient, which may be higher or lower than the badge.','<b>Choose the action</b> — stop, modify, monitor or override — and record the justification.','<b>Read the feedback</b> before moving on, including the consequence where a decision would have caused harm.','<b>Work the queue</b> until every alert is handled or the shift ends; unhandled alerts are scored as dismissed without review.','<b>Review the 24-hour outcomes</b> and the alert-by-alert record, then write and submit your shift report.'],
    method:['Read the badge last, not first — form your own view from the record, then see whether the system agrees.','For every interaction alert ask the same three questions: at what dose, for how long, and in a patient with what current numbers.','Open the laboratory section on any alert involving anticoagulation, renal clearance or electrolytes; that is where the under-graded alerts hide.','Treat an unreconciled medication list as a red flag in both directions — it generates false positives and conceals true duplications.','Reserve “stop the order” for absolute contraindications and active harm; overusing it makes you the obstacle rather than the safeguard.','Override confidently when the alert genuinely does not apply, but always record why — an unexplained override is indistinguishable from a reflex click.','Watch your own fatigue meter. If it is climbing, you are deciding faster than you are investigating.','Remember that delaying necessary treatment through an inappropriate block is a harm, and it is scored as one.']
   },
